# =============================================================================
# DeepSeek-V4-Flash MXFP4 at 131072 -- experts stream to the GPU, not the CPU
# =============================================================================
# A DIFFERENT PRINCIPLE from every other profile here, not another placement.
#
# The others decide WHERE weights live. This one decides WHO COMPUTES THEM. The
# expert weights that do not fit in VRAM still live in host RAM, exactly as in
# the kvram profile -- but they are shipped to the GPU each forward pass and the
# GEMM runs there, instead of running on the CPU.
#
# ggml offloads host-resident tensor ops to a device by default (the op_offload
# mask in ggml-backend.cpp is initialised to all-ones). Every other profile here
# defeats that with -rtr, which repacks the host experts into MXFP4_R8 -- a type
# that exists only under ggml/src/iqk/ and has no CUDA kernel, so the work has
# nowhere to go but the CPU. Dropping -rtr hands it back to the GPU.
#
#   MEASURED 2026-08-17 (RESULTS §21), against the shipped kvram profile:
#
#     depth      kvram (-rtr, ub 1024)   this profile      factor
#     4 101          478.0 pp                1 329.4 pp     2.78x
#     32 701         486.4 pp                1 793.6 pp     3.69x
#     127 981        436.7 pp                1 327.7 pp     3.04x
#
#   Generation is 3-7 % LOWER (19.98 / 19.14 / 17.20 against 21.70 / 20.32 /
#   17.97). That is the trade, and at ~3x prefill it is a cheap one. Nothing is
#   given up on quality: same file, same quant, same arithmetic -- executed on the
#   other processor.
#
#   Those figures are the SWEPT configuration (§21 plus TODO 11 steps 1-3); the
#   first measurement of this profile, before tuning, was 1375.7 / 1529.0 / 1145.4.
#
#   ./serve.sh deepseek-v4-flash-gpu-experts-128k
#   ./serve-deepseek-v4-flash-mxfp4-gpu-experts-128k.sh
#
# WHEN THE OTHER PRINCIPLE WINS. This one ships weights over PCIe every batch, so
# it needs a wide link and a fast GPU, and it wants a large -ub to amortise the
# transfer. -rtr wins on a narrow link or with a CPU strong enough to do the GEMM
# (a Zen 4/5 with AVX-512 would be a different story -- this box is an Arrow Lake
# with AVX2 only). On a PCIe 4.0 x4 link the same strategy collapses.
#
# TUNED, but NOT YET THE DEFAULT. Placement, batching and KV placement have all
# been swept (TODO 11 steps 1-3) and the settings below are the result, not the
# borrowed starting point. What is still missing is a stability soak: the
# intermittent NaN-logits abort (RESULTS §19, TODO 9) was characterised entirely
# in the -rtr CPU path, this is a different one, and shipping an unproven default
# is the mistake that cost two days already.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Same MXFP4 quant as every other DeepSeek profile (~145.6 GiB, 4-file split).
: "${IK_MODEL_GLOB:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/lmstudio-community/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-GGUF/DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731-MXFP4-00001-of-*.gguf}"

: "${IK_ALIAS:=deepseek-v4-flash-gpu-experts-128k}"
: "${IK_CTX:=131072}"

# --- the flag that defines this profile --------------------------------------
# 0 is the whole point. Setting it to 1 turns this into the kvram profile with
# worse batch sizes.
: "${IK_RTR:=0}"

# --- placement ---------------------------------------------------------------
# SWEPT 2026-08-17. Lower is better here, monotonically -- each layer pushed to
# host RAM costs 1.9 % prefill and 2.8 % generation, because it is bytes over the
# link every batch rather than CPU work. So this wants to sit at the floor of what
# fits, and the floor is set by -ub: 17 and 16 both OOM at -ub 4096, and -ub 8192
# needs 19. n19 is that floor for the batch sizes below, not a preference.
: "${IK_FIT:=0}"
: "${IK_NCMOE:=19}"

# --- batching: large on purpose ----------------------------------------------
# Streaming weights costs the same bytes whether the micro-batch holds 512 tokens
# or 8192, so a big -ub is what makes the strategy pay. This is the opposite of
# the kvram profile, where -ub 2048 is implicated in an abort and -ub 1024 ships.
#
# SWEPT 2026-08-17, at 4k / 32k / 128k depth:
#
#   -ub 2048  n18   1029 / 1123 /  --
#   -ub 4096  n18   1371 / 1521 / 1145
#   -ub 6144  n18   loads, serves 4k, then OOMs at 32k
#   -ub 8192  n19   1329 / 1794 / 1328     <-- shipped
#   -ub 12288 n20   OOMs at depth
#   -ub 16384 n21   OOMs at depth
#
# 8192 loses 3.4 % at 4k -- where a 4101-token prompt is one micro-batch either
# way, so the larger setting shows only its cost -- and gains 17.3 % at 32k and
# 15.9 % at 128k. Worth it, and it pays for the extra expert layer several times.
#
# -b matters too, but only at depth: identical to -b 4096 at 4k (0.08 %) and
# +5.9 % at 32k, since a 32k prompt is four logical batches at 8192 and eight at
# 4096. RESULTS §2 called -b inert; that was the -rtr path, where CPU GEMM buried
# it.
#
# DO NOT raise these further without re-checking at depth. The reported compute
# buffer follows 0.859 MiB per unit of -ub, but the runtime allocator wants far
# more: 12288 was given 4493 MiB of headroom by the arithmetic and still died in
# cuMemCreate at 32k, while 8192 survived on 4747. A config can load, report its
# buffer, serve a 4k prompt and then fail deep.
: "${IK_UBATCH:=8192}"
: "${IK_BATCH:=8192}"

# MLA cache is an already-compressed latent: f16, never q8_0.
: "${IK_CTK:=f16}"
: "${IK_CTV:=f16}"

# --- KV in host RAM: not a preference, a requirement -------------------------
# SWEPT 2026-08-17 and the answer is that it cannot be dropped. With the KV back
# on the GPU the CUDA0 compute buffer goes from 3520 MiB to 28 992 at -ub 4096 --
# 8.24x, the attention scratch following the KV home -- and --n-cpu-moe 19, 20 and
# 22 all fail to load. Fitting it would need -ub below ~1150, which throws away
# the amortisation this whole profile depends on. -amb 512 changes neither the
# allocation nor the speed; it does not reach this path.
#
# That also explains the gap to multi-gpu-llm-toolkit rather than closing it: it
# runs --n-cpu-moe 18 with KV on the GPU AND -ub 4096 inside the same 96 GiB, so
# upstream's attention scratch is far smaller than ik_llama's. That is an engine
# difference, not something a flag here can reach.
: "${IK_EXTRA_ARGS:=-mla 3 -fidx --reasoning-format deepseek -nkvo}"
